From: gbagate
Subject: [PATCH] Sessions of up to four players, for Multi-Play links

Base: Celio-Link/Celio-Server 8f509bd with 0001.

A Game Boy Advance Multi-Play link joins up to four GBAs (Pokemon's Cable
Club multi battles, Berry Blender, the record corner; Mario Kart Super
Circuit; and more). Sessions were fixed at two: everything went "to the
opposite client". This lets a session be created for 2 to 4 players, and
leaves a two-player session exactly as it was on the wire.

- sessionCreate takes an optional {players: 2..4}. Clients today send null
  or nothing: two players. Anything else invalid is refused (Invalid
  request). The create/join reply adds `players` and `slot` (the order of
  joining, 0 = the creator).
- "partnerJoined" means the session is complete: with two players, sent to
  the first as before; with more, to everyone waiting once the last joins.
  "partnerLeft" likewise, before the link has started; once it has, a
  leaver still ends it for everyone.
- Roles: a two-player session as before (first ready master, the other
  slave). A Multi-Play link has one parent GBA and up to three children;
  in GB-Link's terms the parent's board answers as the children
  (SetModeSlave) and each child's board drives the link as the parent
  (SetModeMaster). So with more than two, the first ready gets SetModeSlave
  and link ID 0, the others SetModeMaster and IDs 1-3, and every command to
  a player carries its `id`. A board asking again keeps its ID.
- The handshake starts only when the session is full and every player has
  one, and for all of them; ConnectLink goes to all the others.
- Each player's data goes to every other player, through each receiver's
  own PipelinedSender, deduplicated by sequence per sender as before. In a
  session of more than two each packet carries `from`, the sender's link
  ID, so a receiver can put each word in its Multi-Play slot and keep each
  sender's order; a two-player session's packets are unchanged.

test/session.test.ts (new, 16 tests): the options; a two-player session as
before (roles, no link IDs, packets unchanged); a four-player session: full
at four and a fifth refused, told only when complete, one parent and three
children with their link IDs (kept on a repeat), no handshake until all
four, ConnectLink to the other three, every player's data to the other
three once and in order marked with the sender's link ID, a leave before
and after the start. The three two-player tests pass with and without the
change; the other 13 fail without it.

